<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horst-Wessel-Lied</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1929">1929</a>; party anthem <a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Horst-Wessel-Lied&quot; was the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> (NSDAP) from 1930 to 1945, and from 1933 to 1945 the Nazi regime made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s second national anthem, sung after the first stanza of the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a>&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Nazi regime (1933–1945) only the first stanza was sung, followed by the party's &quot;<b>Horst-Wessel-Lied</b>&quot;; three thousand Germans sang it as Hitler entered the stadium at the opening of the 1936 Berlin Olympics.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Horst-Wessel-Lied</b>&quot; was the anthem of the Nazi Party (NSDAP) from 1930 to 1945, and from 1933 to 1945 the Nazi regime made it Germany's second national anthem, sung after the first stanza of the &quot;Deutschlandlied&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li><li><blockquote>The German-controlled Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia (1939–1945) kept the song as its anthem, usually played together with the German anthems, the Deutschlandlied and the Nazi <b>Horst-Wessel-Lied</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kde-domov-muj">Kde domov můj</a></li></ul></section>
